#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: fence-attr-code
---

# An attributed code fence is still code

CommonMark says the info string's FIRST word is the language and the rest is
attributes. The native lane used to compare the WHOLE info string against
`scalascript`, so any attributed fence fell through to "not a code fence" and was
dropped — code included. `def helper` below simply vanished, and the reference to it
from the next block failed with `unbound global: helper` on the native lane while INT
and JS printed `42`.

Regression for `BUGS.md#v2-native-front-drops-attributed-code-fence`. Keep this case:
it is the only corpus case that attributes a `scalascript` fence, which is exactly why
the differential gate could not see the bug.

```scalascript @id=defs
def helper(): Int = 42
```

The definition above must be visible here — same module, attributed fence or not.

```scalascript
println(helper())
```

An attribute on the second fence must not change that either.

```scalascript @id=more
def twice(n: Int): Int = n + n
println(twice(helper()))
```
